// Reference/documentation copy of the line-colors schema — NOT loaded by
// anything (frontend/src/cities.ts and pipeline/seed_line_colors.py both
// read config/line-colors.json, your project's real, live overrides). This
// file uses // comments and is not strict JSON — copy what you need into
// config/line-colors.json by hand instead.
//
// A per-line color override, keyed by city slug then by the line's
// shortName (routes.json's "shortName", the same value shown in
// LineBadge/StopRow/results). Always wins over everything else - checked
// before cities.json's useOfficialLineColors and before the framework's
// own hash-based fallback palette (frontend/src/composables/useLineColor.ts).
// Both colors are used as-is in light AND dark theme (same as an official
// GTFS route_color) - there's no separate dark-mode variant, unlike the
// hash palette.
//
// Lines you don't list here keep using the normal resolution order
// (official GTFS color if useOfficialLineColors is true and the feed has
// one, else the automatic hash palette) - you only need an entry for a
// line you actually want to override.
//
// Getting a starting point instead of hand-picking every hex code:
//   make line-colors CITY=vigo
// seeds one entry per line that doesn't already have one, using whatever
// color it shows today (official if available, else the same hash-palette
// color the frontend would compute) - edit/delete whichever entries you
// want, re-running the command only ever fills in what's still missing
// (new lines added later), never touches or overwrites what you've already
// hand-edited.

{
  "example-city-a": {
    // Deliberately not this line's official GTFS color (or not on an
    // official-gtfs city at all) - a hand-picked brand color instead.
    "7": { "bg": "#7c3aed", "text": "#ffffff" },
    "12": { "bg": "#0a8a5a", "text": "#ffffff" }
  }
}
